# DivvyUp Code Quality Standards

Adapted from the full guide in `cursorrules` (repo root). The original targets a
different stack (React Query, shadcn); these are the parts that apply here.

## Core principles

- Zero duplicate code: search the codebase before writing new functionality;
  extract shared logic to `src/lib/` immediately.
- Consistent patterns: follow existing conventions (design tokens from
  `src/lib/tokens.ts`, pure business logic in `src/lib/`, UI in components).
- Refactor as you code; don't leave debt "for later".

## React

- Prefer derived values (`useMemo`) over `useEffect` + `setState` syncing.
- `useEffect` only for real side effects (subscriptions, API calls, DOM).
- Prefer event handlers over effects for user interactions.
- Stable, unique `key` props on lists (never array index for dynamic lists).
- Split components over ~300 lines; extract hooks/sub-components.

## Design system

- Use `T`, `inputStyle`, `labelStyle`, `cardStyle`, `secTitle` from
  `src/lib/tokens.ts`. Never hardcode colors, radii, shadows, or fonts.

## TypeScript

- No `any` (use `unknown` + narrowing); comment the exception if unavoidable.
- `interface` for object shapes, `type` for unions.
- Explicit return types on exported functions.

## Error handling

- Every async write must surface failures to the user (see
  `persistGroupDiff` + save-error toast pattern in `src/app/page.tsx`).
- User-friendly messages; no raw technical errors in UI.
- No `console.log` in production code (`console.error`/`warn` ok).

## Testing

- Business logic in `src/lib/` gets Vitest coverage (`*.test.ts` colocated).
- Test behavior, not implementation details.

## Accessibility

- Semantic HTML; `aria-label` on icon-only buttons; keyboard accessible.

## Git

- Conventional commits: `type(scope): subject` (feat, fix, docs, refactor,
  perf, test, chore).
